"Around 1798, Beethoven began to lose his hearing, as he told a friend in 1815. The decline was gradual, and it came with a severe form of tinnitus, a ringing in the ear. Historians are not completely sure why it happened. One of his students reported that he could still hear speech and music normally until 1812. Contrary to common belief, Beethoven never became totally deaf. In his final years he could still distinguish low tones and sudden loud sounds.": "badd0a6ed9c8",
"Beethoven’s hearing loss did not prevent him from composing music. From 1814 he used ear trumpets for conversation. Later his hearing seriously deteriorated, and he and the people talking to him wrote in notebooks, called conversation books. His late period, 1812 to 1827, included some of his most innovative works. His Ninth Symphony was first performed on May 7, 1824, in Vienna. He stood by the conductor, beating time, though the conductor had told the performers to follow him instead. A singer turned him around to see the applause he could not hear.": "90d73e9a4396",
"Hearing loss can be partial or total.": "7828167c9e2e",
"Hearing loss is either a partial inability to hear, or a total inability called deafness. Evelyn Glennie, a Scottish percussionist, has been profoundly deaf since childhood, and she performs as a soloist with many orchestras. She plays barefoot to feel the music, and she says deafness is largely misunderstood by the public. For us, the lesson is about reading music and imagining it. It is also a reminder to protect our ears: keep headphones at no more than 60% of maximum, and limit personal audio players to an hour a day.": "c14c166bf348",
"Ludwig van Beethoven was a German composer and pianist. He was baptized on December 17, 1770, in Bonn, and he died on March 26, 1827. There is no authentic record of the date of his birth. He was a musical prodigy, and at age 21 he moved to Vienna, which became his base. Our Lesson 13 shows where he fits between the Classical and the Romantic.": "b00dd1a00ad1",
"Musical notation is any system used to visually represent music, and the process of interpreting it is often called reading music. Sheet music is a written or printed form of musical notation that uses symbols to indicate pitches, rhythms, or chords. The word score is a common alternative term. On a staff, a set of horizontal lines, usually five, each line and space represents a different pitch. Lesson 5 shows how to read it.": "359a465bc5c8",
"Musical notation is any system used to visually represent music, and the process of interpreting it is often called reading music. Sheet music is a written or printed form of musical notation that uses symbols to indicate pitches, rhythms, or chords. The word score is a common alternative term. On a staff, a set of horizontal lines, usually five, each line and space represents a different pitch. Lesson 5 shows how to read it. Sight-reading is the practice of reading and performing a piece in music notation. Highly skilled musicians can sight-read silently: they look at the printed music and hear it in their heads without playing or singing. Audiation is a term coined by Edwin Gordon in 1975 for comprehension and internal realization of music, the sensation of hearing sound when it is not physically present. Skilled composers and conductors have this ability, with Beethoven being a noted historical example. Ludwig van Beethoven was a German composer and pianist. He was baptized on December 17, 1770, in Bonn, and he died on March 26, 1827. There is no authentic record of the date of his birth. He was a musical prodigy, and at age 21 he moved to Vienna, which became his base. Our Lesson 13 shows where he fits between the Classical and the Romantic. Around 1798, Beethoven began to lose his hearing, as he told a friend in 1815. The decline was gradual, and it came with a severe form of tinnitus, a ringing in the ear. Historians are not completely sure why it happened. One of his students reported that he could still hear speech and music normally until 1812. Contrary to common belief, Beethoven never became totally deaf. In his final years he could still distinguish low tones and sudden loud sounds. Beethoven’s hearing loss did not prevent him from composing music. From 1814 he used ear trumpets for conversation. Later his hearing seriously deteriorated, and he and the people talking to him wrote in notebooks, called conversation books. His late period, 1812 to 1827, included some of his most innovative works. His Ninth Symphony was first performed on May 7, 1824, in Vienna. He stood by the conductor, beating time, though the conductor had told the performers to follow him instead. A singer turned him around to see the applause he could not hear. Hearing loss is either a partial inability to hear, or a total inability called deafness. Evelyn Glennie, a Scottish percussionist, has been profoundly deaf since childhood, and she performs as a soloist with many orchestras. She plays barefoot to feel the music, and she says deafness is largely misunderstood by the public. For us, the lesson is about reading music and imagining it. It is also a reminder to protect our ears: keep headphones at no more than 60% of maximum, and limit personal audio players to an hour a day.": "e79bcc065aca",
